  "lessons": [
    {
      "title": "DIY Triangle Scarf and Shawls",
      "slug": "diy-triangle-scarf-and-shawls",
      "displayOrder": 1,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 394,
        "lessonOrder": 1
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "These scarves and shawls are easy knitting and can be used as a canvas for your creativity. Knit in lace (automatic or hand manipulated), stitch patterns, or stripes. Consider knitting them on the ribber.<br>This Knit it Now Collection gives you a variety of styles to choose, each with it's own features.<br><b>Triangular scarves</b> are a fun way to try new stitch patterns and create quick gifts or craft fair items using a small amount of yarn. Because they are small, the needlebed limitations of our machines don't come into play as much as with a full shawl.<br>Knit <b>triangular shawls</b> in different directions for a different look.<br>All of these designs are shaped working increases and/or decreases over every-other-row. You don't need to follow a chart and keep track of your row counts. But shaping this way forces the dimensions of your shawl/scarf to specific sizes based on your gauge. <br>",
              "legacyComponentId": 5649,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "408632659",
              "title": null,
              "legacySource": "embedded-html",
              "legacyComponentId": 5649,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n\t\t\t<b>Once you establish YOUR gauge, it's important to note the finished dimensions of your chosen style based on the number of needles you put into work.</b><br><em> In other words, take time to examine the diagram for the finished dimensions BEFORE you start knitting.</em><br>\r\n\t\tThe patterns also include <b>yarn calculations</b> based on your swatch. Even though you are knitting \"just a shawl\" is critical that you knit and dress your swatch and build your pattern based on the accurate measurements of your gauge.<br>\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"table-responsive\">\r\n\t\t\t\t\t<table class=\"table-striped table\">\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<thead>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th></th>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th></th>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th>Notes</th>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th>Scarf</th>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th>Shawl</th>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</thead>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<tbody>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td><img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/60/icon_triangle.jpg\">\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\r\n\r\n\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td><b>Triangle Scarf</b></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td>Knit from the widest edge to the point (using decreases). Stripes run parallel to the cast on edge. Limited Width</td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td align=\"center\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td align=\"center\"><i class=\"fa fa-times\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td> <img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/60/icon_fastneasy.jpg\">\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td><b>Fast 'n Easy</b></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td>Knit from the max needles and decreased to a point, stripes run parallel to the cast on edge. This style may be more appropriate for a scarf or shoulder warmer. Check the dimensions before knitting.</td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td align=\"center\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td align=\"center\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/60/icon_pointtopoint.jpg\">\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td><b>Point-to-Point</b></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td>Start at a point, increase to the max needles, then decrease to a point. This style allows for a wider shawl (fingertip to fingertip). the back length is limited to the number of needles on the needlebed. Stripes appear vertically at center back</td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td align=\"center\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td align=\"center\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr>\r\n\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td><img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/60/icon_chevron.jpg\">\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\r\n\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td><b>Chevron</b></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td>Knit in 2 pieces, this allows you to create the largest of these styles. Knit from point to point, stripes create a decorative chevron design at the center back. </td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td align=\"center\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td align=\"center\"><i class=\"fa fa-check\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></td>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</tbody>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t</table>\r\n\t\t\t\t</div>\r\n\t\t\t\r\n\r\n\t\t\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 5818,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "About Triangle Scarves / Shawls",
          "slug": "about-triangle-scarves-shawls",
