    {
      "title": "Fast 'n Easy",
      "slug": "fast-n-easy",
      "displayOrder": 3,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 388,
        "lessonOrder": 3
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\n\tOur machines have a limited number of needles, which limits the width of our knitting. We have to be creative in planning to knit large pieces.<br>This one-piece design can be used for a scarf or shawl. <br>It is designed for easy knitting with decreases every-other-row, forming the triangle shape.<br>If adding stripes, using self-striping yarn or adding horizontal stitch pattern, the pattern will run parallel to the cast on edge.<br><em><i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i><b>TIP:</b><br> For even faster knitting, flip this around. Start with 1-3 stitches and INCREASE every-other-row\r\n\t\t Use a <a data-vimeoid=\"151858451\" class=\"videopopup\"><i class=\"fa fa-youtube-play\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> KISS increase</a> on the carriage side EVERY ROW.<br>\r\n\t\t</em><br>\r\n\t\r\n\t<img src=\"/path/images/158/easy_version_as_worn.jpg\">\r\n\r\nNOTE: The shaping is worked over every-other-row. Depending on your stitch pattern, the shawl may not be symmetrical. If the difference is too great, you may want to work a different shaping sequence. Use the Magic Formula to calculate your shaping instructions",
              "legacyComponentId": 5611,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Fast 'n Easy Shawl",
          "slug": "fast-n-easy-shawl",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
